To store the hexadecimal value \(5\text{D}\text{H}\) (\(01011101_2\)) into the flag register of the 8085 microprocessor without using any arithmetic instructions and ensuring no other registers are altered, we must manipulate the processor status word (\(\text{PSW}\)) using the stack. The \(\text{PSW}\) register pair consists of the Accumulator (\(A\), high byte) and the Flag register (\(F\), low byte). \[\boxed{
    \begin{array}{l}
    \text{PUSH B} \quad \text{; Save original BC pair onto the stack} \\
    \text{PUSH PSW} \,\, \text{; Save original Accumulator and Flags} \\
    \text{POP B} \quad \,\, \text{; Move original A into B, and original F into C} \\
    \text{MVI C, 5DH} \,\, \text{; Load desired flag value 5DH into register C} \\
    \text{PUSH B} \quad \text{; Push original A (now in B) and new F (in C) as PSW} \\
    \text{POP PSW} \quad \text{; Pop the values into Accumulator and Flag register} \\
    \text{POP B} \quad \,\, \text{; Restore the original BC pair from the stack}
    \end{array}
    }\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>The \(\text{PSW}\) pair is organized as \(A\) (MSB) and \(F\) (LSB).</li><li>Utilizing an alternate register pair like \(BC\) allows temporary retention of the Accumulator's data so it remains unaltered after execution.</li></ul></div> <div class="sol-block sol-mistakes"><div class="sol-block-title">Mistakes to avoid</div><ul><li>Do not use instructions like MVI A, 5DH directly without preserving and restoring the original contents of the Accumulator.</li></ul></div>

