A very important property of the Fourier Transform is that a Gaussian function remains Gaussian in the frequency domain. If a Gaussian pulse exists in time domain, then its Fourier Transform is also Gaussian in frequency domain. <strong>Gaussian Pulse in Time Domain</strong> \[x(t)=e^{-at^2},
\qquad a&gt;0\]Taking Fourier Transform, \[X(f)
=
\sqrt{\frac{\pi}{a}}
\;e^{-\frac{\pi^2 f^2}{a}}\]which is again a Gaussian function in frequency. \[\boxed{(C) 
\text{Gaussian pulse in time domain }
\;\overset{\mathcal{F}}{\longleftrightarrow}\;
\text{ Gaussian pulse in frequency domain}
}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li><strong>Gaussian Fourier Transform Property:</strong> A narrow pulse in time domain corresponds to a wide spectrum in frequency domain, and vice versa. Gaussian functions preserve their shape under Fourier Transform.</li></ul></div>

